    "durationMs": 229842,
    "finalText": "The URL shortener API is complete and verified. Summary of what I built and found:\n\n**Implementation** (Rails 8.0.2 API-only, PostgreSQL via `DATABASE_URL`):\n- `links` table: `code` (unique index), `url` (text), `clicks` (bigint, default 0) — created via a migration that `start.sh` applies with `bin/rails db:prepare` before serving on `0.0.0.0:8000` in the foreground.\n- `POST /links` — validates url with a custom `UrlValidator` (absolute http/https with host), validates code format (`/\\A[A-Za-z0-9_-]{4,20}\\z/`), generates an 8-char code when omitted, returns 201/409/422 as specified; submitted URL is stored and returned unchanged.\n- `GET /links/{code}`, `GET /links` (ascending creation order with `total`), `GET /r/{code}` — 302 redirect with an atomic single-UPDATE click increment (`increment_counter`, exactly once per request), JSON `error` field on all failures, malformed JSON rescued to 422.\n- `config/database.yml` contains no credentials — everything comes from `DATABASE_URL`.\n\n**Two notable fixes during testing:**\n1. Bundler had resolved the Gemfile's `~> 8.0.2` to Rails 8.0.5.2 — I pinned `rails` to exactly `8.0.2`.\n2. The freshly resolved `json` 3.x gem broke Rails 8.0.2's ActiveSupport (which still passes `quirks_mode:` to `JSON.parse`), causing 500s on every request — fixed by pinning `json` to 2.x.\n\n**Verification:** 35/35 curl checks passed (201/409/422/404/302 paths, boundary 4- and 20-char codes, click counting, list ordering/structure), data and click counts survived a server restart, and a full cold start (database dropped, then `sh start.sh`) recreated the schema from scratch. The database is left empty with 0 rows and port 8000 free, ready for the test harness."
  }
